  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 308 AM EDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nCold advection underway on nw low-level flow, ahead of high\npressure building into MN/WI. Enough moisture and lake\ninstability on Superior to generate lake effect rain showers\ninto eastern upper MI. Lake clouds have been expanding in\nnorthern lower MI, but the moisture depth is shallower, and \nlittle if any precip down here.\n\nUpstream high pressure will be over western sections of the\nforecast by dusk today. Mid-level subsidence and drying will\nincrease today, lowering inversion heights. Sct lake effect \nrain showers will continue in the eastern UP this morning, \nmainly early. Those will shut down, though the eastern UP \nremains on the mostly cloudy side. Northern lower MI will \neventually mix out to partly-mostly sunny this afternoon. \n\nHigh pressure continues toward the Mid-Atlantic coast, where it\nstalls for a couple day before heading further east. Southerly\nlow-level return flow begins Saturday, and continues until... \nuntil something moves in from the west and disrupts it. That\nwon't be until the middle part of next week. However, the\ntendency for isentropic ascent aloft to generate precip will be\nfighting off rising heights, at least thru the end of the\nweekend. By Sunday, a shortwave ridge will arrive overhead. The\nbuilding ridge should win out, most of the time, in most of the\narea. However, an isolated shower may be possible Sat night/ \nSunday for along the immediate nw lower MI coast, on up across \nthe eastern UP. \n\nPops do continue to increase for Monday night and beyond. A\nlarge cyclone is likely to develop in the lee of the central\nRockies, then mature as it ejects toward the northern lakes.\nThis system could well be in a weakening state by the time it \nactually gets here, but abundant moisture will be pushed north \nahead of it. \n\nTemps will be briefly somewhat cooler today, but steadily\nrebound thru the weekend into early next week. Our next cooldown\nlooks most likely late next week? Maybe? We'll see.",
